d you have wire to ensure the  structure of the tire which is simply  recovered as crap metal so nothing is  lost the process is fully circular  that's a huge breakthrough compared to  the current solutions that are proposed  for our used tires  now we've seen in the last week the  energy crisis caused scenes of violence  and unrest on the streets of kazakhstan  lifting a price cap on liquefied  petroleum gas used in vehicles and  households saw the cost of the fuel  double and triggered huge protests  governments around the world are faced  with the challenge of moving from fossil  fuels to greener energy which inevitably  means reducing the subsidies which keep  fuel prices down  so what can be done to help the public  cope with the cost of the change well  joining us now is mika migno paluello  climate and industry lead at the trades  union congress thanks so much for  talking to us today  so protests in kazakhstan sparked there  by rise in energy prices to what extent  is this global push towards greener  energy  playing a role in that price rise  thanks for having me um  i'd actually argue that the problem is  largely about the extent to which  kazakhstan has remained hooked on fossil  fuels rather than the transition  the protest against the kazakh petra  state regime was sparked by the global  gas price rise  um that we've seen whether in the uk or  in kazakhstan millions of people are now  having to choose whether to whether to  heat their whether to heat their homes  or to eat and while  at the same time ruling elites and  corporations are walking away with  billions of pounds kazakh gas has  exported to um to china to produce  consumer goods ultimately that we import  if instead we had deployed renewable  energy at scale globally  publicly owned 20 years ago as the  climate science told us to do  then we'd be much less susceptible to  the volatile prices that we're seeing  today we wouldn't be  having to you wouldn't see large  populations slapped with thi