et zero  investment in order to pay for those  rising energy costs and so sam to you  first of all a number of your tory mp  colleagues are worried that green levees  on our fuel bills which are designed of  course to help finance the transition  away from fossil fuels are adding an  unnecessary burden on people when their  bills are already going up should those  levies be paused do you think so that  people don't have to choose between  eating and heating their home  well the first thing to say about this  is that the the main cause of the energy  bill spike that we're seeing at the  moment is the wholesale price of energy  in particular the um wholesale cost of  gas which has increased by between 400  and 500 in europe over the last year and  that's not really something that the uk  government can do anything about is to  do with vladimir putin constraining gas  supplies uh to europe it's to do with  asian economies demanding more gas and  therefore there being more competition  for liquefied national gas gas shipments  so  that bit of it there's not really much  that the government can do in the short  term in terms of levies um i definitely  do not think that we should be or could  even pause some of those um  for starters a lot of the levies are  underpinned by legally binding contracts  the contracts the government's entered  in in order to support investment in  clean energy but they also support a lot  of jobs as well the uk's world leading  offshore wind sectors built off the back  of some of these contracts and we  wouldn't want to undermine them but  longer term we know that the only  solution to this crisis which is based  on our dependence on gas is to get away  from gas and to shift towards clean  energy and it's precisely this  investment in clean energy which these  levy support which will which will get  us there which will make us less  reliable  so judy do you think this is the best  way to to pay for that transition though  i mean centrica's ceo said recently  strip environmental and social levies  out of energy bills and fund green  programs through general taxation do you  think that could be a better route  i think we need to think about all  aspects of how we think about becoming  greener when people understand that  certain things we have to pay more for  in order not to damage the earth and  have a better life they're actually  happy to pay more it is about what it is  and why the price rise is going the  other thing is about equity you know we  need to put into place frameworks that  are fair in society recently i've been  actually proposing thinking about a  green minimum wage for example i have  colleagues and so on who are not very  poor but they constantly complain about  how they would like to be greener they  really want to be greener but they just  can't afford to do so we need to begin  to envisage a future for all of society  in which we can all participate and pay  for the things where we want it to be  greener  interesting stuff and sam we talk about  the cost to consumers of their energy  but clearly cost of business is very  high too especially in these energy  intensive industries and a third of them  in this survey have talked about calling  off some of their environmental  investments to deal with the crisis i  know that labour is talking about a  contingency fund to support those  struggling firms would you back that  i mean i i certainly think the  government should look at some  short-term support for households and  for business and we just discussed their  some of the levies i think rather than  pausing them or scrapping them off bills  entirely what the government could do  instead is fund some of them out of  general taxation and i think um that  would at least on a short-term basis  provide some relief um to businesses and  also on all households uh it was going  to struggle with the cost of energy and  i think that sort of measure might might  well be better than that than t