 said recently  strip environmental and social levies  out of energy bills and fund green  programs through general taxation do you  think that could be a better route  i think we need to think about all  aspects of how we think about becoming  greener when people understand that  certain things we have to pay more for  in order not to damage the earth and  have a better life they're actually  happy to pay more it is about what it is  and why the price rise is going the  other thing is about equity you know we  need to put into place frameworks that  are fair in society recently i've been  actually proposing thinking about a  green minimum wage for example i have  colleagues and so on who are not very  poor but they constantly complain about  how they would like to be greener they  really want to be greener but they just  can't afford to do so we need to begin  to envisage a future for all of society  in which we can all participate and pay  for the things where we want it to be  greener  interesting stuff and sam we talk about  the cost to consumers of their energy  but clearly cost of business is very  high too especially in these energy  intensive industries and a third of them  in this survey have talked about calling  off some of their environmental  investments to deal with the crisis i  know that labour is talking about a  contingency fund to support those  struggling firms would you back that  i mean i i certainly think the  government should look at some  short-term support for households and  for business and we just discussed their  some of the levies i think rather than  pausing them or scrapping them off bills  entirely what the government could do  instead is fund some of them out of  general taxation and i think um that  would at least on a short-term basis  provide some relief um to businesses and  also on all households uh it was going  to struggle with the cost of energy and  i think that sort of measure might might  well be better than that than that  proposal you mentioned  and judy do you think that the people  are ready to make sacrifices and  lifestyle changes you talked about how  you discuss these issues with people do  you think that people are changing and  they are more open to it  well right from the experience of  covered lockdown and so on as you know  there's been a shift in attitude about  how we look at life many people have  developed an attitude of centering on  the essentials walking in nature which  is free  and having the pleasure of being with  close family colleagues and communities  their values are changing and so in a  way a lot of my middle class friends  actually said to me we didn't go to  theater we don't go to football and so  on and in lockdown there was money  piling up in our bank which made us  realize we have lots of spare money  besides the essentials so in a way  they're also shifting to us and this is  something government might like to think  about a lot of middle class people would  be happier to pay more tax to have a  better life rather than just lots of  spare cash things are really changing in  terms of how we look into this green  future and we need more information i  think the daily climate show is very  important because you give out lots of  information that combines of aspiration  to shift us towards better ways of  behaving and thinking about where we put  our money and what we want to be taxed  okay i don't want to cut you off when  you're being nice about the daily  climate show but we really ought to move  on to our next topic  which is about a bill gates backed fund  to which is preparing to invest billions  of dollars in clean tech projects such  as direct air capture green hydrogen and  aviation fuel now is this part of the  solution private capital stepping in  where institutions don't see immediate  profit or would that money be better  spent on the things that we know are  important but perhaps not as exciting  like improving public transport perhaps  or insulating our homes so sam do you  welcome this private investment into  technological research i mean how much  of a role should private capital be  playing or really should it be the job  of governments to fund developments that  will benefit us all in the long run  i mean i think private capital uh has  has a huge role and we know that this  transition to net zero globally is going  to be very capital intensive it's going  to require lots of new infrastructure  lots of new um techniques and  forms of businesses which we know will  require investment and it's too much i  think for any government  to fund and to finance exclusively so  the private sector has got a really key  role and so i absolutely welcome welcome  this fund and i think you know you're  right to say that there are some easy  readily deployable uh solutions which  are available now things like wind solar  which have come down a lot in cost in  recent decades it's absolutely right  that we keep deploying those at scale  but we do also need new technologies and  cheaper technologies and some of the  hard to decarbonize sectors like  aviation like the negative emissions so  that's where i think the real value of  this fund will come from  and technology judy does have a big part  to play in tackling climate change  doesn't it potentially but do you think  the money is heading in the right  direction i mean talking about aviation  fuel for example do you think that's the  right area that we should be focusing on  at the moment  i really want the massive amounts of  money floating around going into things  that are really fundamental to the  center of people's lives you talk about  massive investment initiatives focusing  on lowering green premiums on aviation  does not appeal to me because it's not  sustainable to encourage more flying and  some time ago i heard that the jet  setting habits of bill gates and paris  hilton mean that they produce an  astonishing 10 000 times more carbon  emissions from flying than an average  person i think we need to think  differently about funding these things  such as green premiums maybe all  first-class seats on the plane and  landing fees will private jets and in  the airport should be massively  expensive so that it covers all green  premiums for everybody okay i don't  think it's fair for a lot of you know  ordinary people to pay for it a quick  reaction to that sam what do you think  i mean i i i think that technology uh  is is essential in sectors like aviation  because we know that when you told  people about things they're willing to  do that in their own lives to tackle  climate change flying less eating less  meat and dairy it's going to appeal to  some people some people want to make  those personal decisions for them to be  more sustainable but it doesn't appeal  to a lot of people and i think we need  to instead design develop commercialize  the technology which will enable people  to keep doing those things but in a  sustainable zero carbon way and so um  you know i think we need to put in place  the financing the incentives to get  those technologies to market  okay well sam hall judy ling one we must  leave it there but thank you both very  much indeed thank  you well tomorrow i'm going to be joined  by co-founder of the green new deal uk  fatima ibrahim and john verdieg the  director of international climate policy  at the nature conservancy in the us  thanks for watching see you next time  [Music]
 

